arned  here in the united states senate  roughly 180 in the house of  representatives and thousands of small  businesses in  indiana and across the country were  supportive of this uh to the extent we  need additional  small business relief and you've  indicated we might is this something uh  that you might work with me and senator  bennett  uh to uh try and perfect and perhaps  implement in a bipartisan way  absolutely i would look forward to  working with you on that  thank you so much thank you senator  young  senator's ass thank you mr chairman  dr yellen congratulations on your  nomination  obviously an important capstone to a  very distinguished career  um i want to focus on a set of topics  that involve the national security  equities of the treasury department more  than some of the topics of the last  couple  of questions it is my view that whenever  the senate is fulfilling our advice and  consent obligations  with regard to any senior position that  has national security equities  one of our obligations is to make sure  that the nominee in question regards  herself or himself  as having a national security portfolio  uh and secondarily  i think it's it's clear that there is a  bipartisan  consensus in the senate now i come to  you having just been an  intelligence committee hearing first  open and then closed  and the bipartisan consensus is  overwhelming uh that the number one  long-term geopolitical  threat the united states faces not just  in the next four years but probably in  the next four decades  is the chinese communist party so i want  to ask you two questions about that  first  dr yellen do you believe that the  secretary of the treasury is a national  security position  uh in the in the president's cabinet  absolutely i think treasury along with  um the rest of the administration needs  to stay laser focused on that  we have many tools that are implemented  through treasury to address  national security concerns the treasury  heads the leads t